<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apache</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apache are a group of culturally related Indigenous peoples of the American Southwest and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, speaking Southern Athabaskan languages. Arriving from the north by about 1500, they include the Chiricahua, Mescalero, Jicarilla, Lipan and Western Apache. For more than two centuries their raids challenged Spanish, Mexican and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-frontier">American frontier</a> settlements, and leaders such as Cochise and Geronimo resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> until 1886.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apache.jpg" alt="Apache" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robfergusonjr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apache_portraits.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Apache are several Southern Athabaskan-speaking peoples of the Southwest, the Southern Plains and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, linguistically related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a>. The six major groups are the Chiricahua, Jicarilla, Lipan, Mescalero, Plains and Western Apache. Anthropological evidence suggests that the Apache and Navajo lived in the north, where other Athabaskan speakers still live, before migrating to the Southwest sometime between 1200 and 1500 CE. Their homelands, known collectively as Apacheria, include high mountains, canyons, deserts and the southern Great Plains. The word Apache is Spanish; the first known written record is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> in 1598, and the most widely accepted theory is that it comes from the Zuni word meaning Navajos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Apache fought the invading Spanish and Mexican peoples for centuries, though Spanish colonists and Apache bands also traded with each other; a band might be friendly with one village and raid another. The first Apache raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> seem to date from the late 17th century. As tensions grew, the Mexican government passed laws offering cash rewards for Apache scalps. In the 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-indian-wars">American Indian Wars</a> the United States Army found the Apache to be fierce warriors and skilful strategists.</p><p>In 1875 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military forced the removal of about 1,500 Yavapai and Tonto Apache from the Rio Verde reserve to San Carlos, 180 miles away, on a march through flooded rivers and mountain passes that killed several hundred people. They were interned there for 25 years while settlers took their land. Most United States accounts place the final defeat of an Apache band on 4 September 1886, when 5,000 troops forced Geronimo's group of 30 to 50 men, women and children to surrender at Skeleton Canyon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>. The army sent them and the Chiricahua scouts who had tracked them to confinement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-sill">Fort Sill</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Before the reservations Apache lived in extended family units, usually of a couple, their unmarried children, their married daughters and those daughters' husbands and children, living near one another. Housing varied: tipis on the plains, wickiups of wood and brush in the highlands, and earthen hogans in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Food came from hunting, gathering, trade, raiding and, among some groups, cultivation. The Apache distinguished raiding, done by small parties for a specific economic purpose, from war, waged by large parties usually for retribution.</p><p>Each Apache tribe is today politically autonomous, with tribes and reservations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, and Apache settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a>, Chihuahua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Their languages are endangered, and Lipan is reported extinct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apache” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following decades the Comanche became mounted bison hunters and drove the Jicarilla, Lipan and other <b>Apache</b> groups from the grasslands east of the Rocky Mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On the northern frontier, a line of presidios, or garrisoned forts, protected missions and mining towns against <b>Apache</b> and Comanche raids, and the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 drove the Spaniards out of New Mexico until Diego de Vargas returned in 1692.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 1500 Athapaskan-speaking peoples, the ancestors of the Navajo and <b>Apache</b>, had entered the region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Their language, Diné bizaad, “the people’s language”, is a Southern Athabaskan language of the Na-Dené family, with two mutually intelligible dialects, and is closely related to the languages of the <b>Apache</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the reforms of José de Gálvez, Charles III separated the northern provinces of New Spain from the viceroy's direct control in 1776 to strengthen frontier defence against the <b>Apache</b> and Comanche.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>Presidios guarded routes against <b>Apache</b> and other raiders, and the Camino Real linked it to Mexico City and Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico.</blockquote><a href="/state/nueva-vizcaya">Nueva Vizcaya</a></li></ul></section>
